  "grammar": {
    "title": "Prepositions of Time: In, On, At",
    "explanation": "Use **at** for a point in time. We say **at** seven o'clock and **at** night.\n\nUse **on** for a day or a date. We say **on** Monday and **on** my birthday.\n\nUse **in** for a longer time. We say **in** July, **in** summer, and **in** 2026.\n\nMy aunt was born **in** July. Her birthday is **on** a Sunday this year.\n\nDo not use **in**, **on**, or **at** before next, last, this, or every. We say next Friday, not **on** next Friday.",
    "examples": [
      "*The film starts **at** 8 pm.* (clock time)",
      "*My birthday is **on** 12 October.* (date)",
      "*I was born **in** 1998.* (year)",
      "*We go skiing **in** winter.* (season)",
      "*I work **on** Mondays and Wednesdays.* (days)",
      "*She always reads **in** the evening.* (part of the day)"
    ],
